{# Shared Jinja2 macros for every M2.2 invoice layout: the print-safe <head> boilerplate (fonts,
   page size, resets) and the two `data-field` wrappers every layout uses to tag canonical
   extraction-schema values, so M2.3 can find them by DOM box (PRD §10 M2.2). #}

{% macro head_common() -%}
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<style>
{{ font_face_css() }}
@page { size: A4; margin: 14mm; }
* { box-sizing: border-box; }
html, body { margin: 0; padding: 0; }
body { -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact; print-color-adjust: exact; }
table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }
img { max-width: 100%; }
.ap-page { break-inside: avoid; }
.ap-row { break-inside: avoid; }
/* M2.4 label self-check fix: `factory/render.py`'s `_PAGINATE_JS` treats every `<header>`,
   `<section>` and `<footer>` landmark that straddles a page boundary as one atomic block to push
   whole onto the next page -- correct only if Chromium's real print engine treats it the same
   way. A landmark with no break-avoidance of its own is free to print split across the boundary
   instead (rendering wherever its content naturally reaches), which the simulation has no way to
   predict; confirmed empirically for a `<footer>` whose own `margin-block-start` pushed it just
   past a page's content zone -- the simulation pushed it whole onto a *third* page that the real
   PDF, having let it split instead, never has (labels landing on a page index the document
   doesn't have, breaking any exact-position check outright). Applying the same break-avoidance
   these landmarks are being *treated* as having makes Chromium's real behavior match the
   simulation instead of the other way around (PRD §10 M2.4).
*/
header, section, footer { break-inside: avoid; }
/* M2.4 label self-check fix: a bare <thead> defaults to `display: table-header-group`, which
   Chromium's print engine repeats at the top of every printed page a table spans -- confirmed
   empirically: reverting this one rule (while keeping every other M2.4 pagination fix) collapsed
   a 3-page bilingual layout's match rate from 100% to 35%, and restoring it brought it straight
   back. That repeated copy consumes real vertical space on page 2+ that `factory/render.py`'s
   `_PAGINATE_JS` (an on-screen DOM simulation that only ever sees the thead once, since nothing
   is really fragmenting on screen) has no way to know about, so it mis-locates everything after
   the first page break by the repeated header's height, compounding page over page. Forcing plain
   `table-row-group` makes the header render once, in normal document flow, matching what the
   on-screen simulation actually measures -- trading the header repeating on later printed pages
   for correct label coordinates there (PRD §10 M2.4).
*/
thead { display: table-row-group; }
/* M2.4 label self-check fix: every footer template ends its payment sentence with
   "...IBAN {{ field_ltr('iban', iban) }}." -- the closing "." directly abuts the IBAN's own span
   with no space (correct prose; a space before a full stop would look wrong), so in the rendered
   PDF the period's glyph can land under 1pt from the IBAN's last digit. That is well inside
   pdfplumber's own sub-pixel rendering jitter, so `factory/selfcheck.py`'s box-boundary distance
   check -- which has to stay loose enough to still catch a digit legitimately clipped at a run
   edge -- occasionally can't tell the period apart from the IBAN it follows, on some layouts but
   not others depending on exact font metrics. A small, visually negligible end margin gives every
   layout a reliable, print-real gap here instead of leaving it to whatever the font happened to
   kern, without changing where the text visibly sits relative to the rest of the sentence (PRD
   §10 M2.4).
*/
[data-field="iban"] { margin-inline-end: 2pt; }
/* M2.4 label self-check / M3.1 IoU-gate fix: a `[data-field]` span's `getClientRects()` box is
   the CSS *line box* it generates, whose height comes from `line-height` (here inherited as
   18px at this table's 12px font -- 1.5x), not the glyph ink of the one short word/number it
   wraps. For a normal multi-word sentence this slack is proportionally small, but for a short,
   single-token field like `currency` ("AED") it dwarfs the actual glyph box, so the tight word
   box pdfplumber/apx.parse measures independently scores a low IoU against it even when
   perfectly centered -- confirmed by measuring both `element.getClientRects()` and
   `Range.getClientRects()` over the same text node: identical, since line-height inflates a
   line box regardless of which of the two you query. `line-height: 1` collapses that box to
   (approximately) the font's own natural metrics -- still not pixel-identical to a font's glyph
   ink, but far closer -- for every field, not just currency, and every span here is a single
   inline run inside a table cell whose own `vertical-align: middle` centering (unaffected by
   this) already governs the visible row height, so this has no visible effect on the rendered
   PDF (PRD §10 M2.4).
*/
[data-field] { line-height: 1; }
/* M2.4 label self-check fix: the bundled Noto Naskh/Kufi Arabic fonts' "ccmp" (glyph
   composition/decomposition) OpenType feature makes Chromium's print-to-PDF path emit a broken
   ToUnicode CMap for some shaped Arabic glyphs -- NUL filler characters plus, for at least
   isolated Teh Marbuta, an outright wrong codepoint. Re-measured once the bundled fonts really
   loaded (they had silently fallen back to system fonts before the `font_face_css` autoescape
   fix): with "ccmp" on, Chromium maps the decomposed glyphs to dotless base letters and Arabic
   extraction drops to 0% on a 300-doc sample; with it off, 100%. The cost is visual: letters
   whose dots or hamza seat come from composition (e.g. the hamza on yeh) render slightly off.
   Required joining ("init"/"medi"/"fina") stays enabled, so letter shapes stay connected. Scoped to `:lang(ar)`, which the
   `lang="ar"` attribute on either the document root or one bilingual sub-block inherits down
   to, so English/Latin text (a different font) is untouched (PRD §10 M2.4).
*/
:lang(ar) { font-feature-settings: "ccmp" 0; }
</style>
{%- endmacro %}

{# A field whose visible text is already in the correct script/direction for the surrounding
   flow (Arabic prose, or English prose in an English layout). #}
{% macro field(name, value, class_='') -%}
<span class="{{ class_ }}" data-field="{{ name }}">{{ value }}</span>
{%- endmacro %}

{# A field whose visible text is Western digits or Latin script (invoice numbers, dates, TRN,
   IBAN, currency code, money amounts, quantities, rates) that must stay left-to-right even when
   embedded in Arabic (RTL) prose. Safe (a no-op) in left-to-right layouts too. #}
{% macro field_ltr(name, value, class_='') -%}
<span class="{{ class_ }}" data-field="{{ name }}">{{ value | ltr }}</span>
{%- endmacro %}

{# A visual-only echo of a value some other `field_ltr(name, ...)` call on the same page already
   tags: a language-invariant field (a code, TRN, IBAN, quantity, amount -- anything with no
   actual translation) that a bilingual layout re-displays in its second language block purely for
   that block's own readability, not a second physical occurrence of the field. Wrapping it in a
   second `data-field="name"` span produced two ground-truth boxes for one extraction-schema
   field, which is what M3.1's IoU gate caught (13 `currency` labels with a low IoU because a
   downstream consumer picked whichever of the two boxes did not match the one `apx.parse` found
   -- PRD §10 M2.4/M3.1). This keeps the same `line-height: 1` box-tightening `[data-field]`
   spans get (so the echo lines up with its row exactly as before) without adding a second label
   for the same field; only a field whose text genuinely differs by language (a name, an address,
   a description) still gets tagged in both blocks. #}
{% macro field_ltr_shadow(value, class_='') -%}
<span class="{{ class_ }}" style="line-height: 1">{{ value | ltr }}</span>
{%- endmacro %}
